            <ArticleTitle>[MALT lymphoma of the prostate: Place of radiotherapy. Case report and discussion].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>746-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.canrad.2015.06.019</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1278-3218(15)00371-6</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We report the case of a patient presenting with MALT (mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue) lymphoma of the prostate, who received an irradiation delivering 30.6 Gy in 17 fractions after transurethral resection. With a follow-up of 6 years, he remained alive and free of disease. In view of the literature and this case report, definitive local irradiation should be administered in this rare disease.</AbstractText>
